#
# Per-Dockerfile ignore list. BuildKit prefers "<dockerfile>.dockerignore" over the context root's
# .dockerignore, so this file REPLACES the root one for `-f examples/chatbot/Dockerfile.mcp` builds
# and has no effect on any other image.
#
# It exists because the root .dockerignore excludes `examples/` wholesale (line 28, under a comment
# claiming "none are COPY'd by any Dockerfile" -- which stopped being true when the chatbot example
# arrived). Without an override the build fails at COPY with "/examples/chatbot/demo_mcp: not
# found", which reads like a typo in the Dockerfile rather than an ignore rule.
#
# Written as deny-all-then-allow rather than as `!examples/chatbot/`, and that is the security-
# relevant half. Excluding a path from the ROOT .dockerignore stops it being COPY'd; it does not
# stop it being SENT to the daemon as part of the context. The root file carries a long comment
# about exactly that risk for `.secrets/`. Starting from `*` means this build's context is two
# paths and nothing else -- no .git, no .venv, no .env, no .secrets/, no node_modules -- so there is
# no credential in it to leak and no future `COPY . .` that could bake one in.
*
!examples/chatbot/requirements-mcp.txt
!examples/chatbot/demo_mcp

# After the re-include, because the last matching pattern wins. The root .dockerignore carries a
# blanket `**/__pycache__` that this file replaced, and re-including a source directory brings the
# developer's bytecode with it -- CPython 3.12 on the host writes .pyc that CPython 3.12 in the
# image will happily load, so a stale cache can outlive an edit to the .py beside it.
examples/chatbot/demo_mcp/**/__pycache__
